<section class="privacy section container" id="privacy" aria-labelledby="privacy-heading"><div class="privacy-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / MAKE AN INFORMED CHOICE</p><h2 id="privacy-heading">Local processing.<br> Clear data boundaries.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/arpanjot-coding/Datly/blob/main/py-src/data_formulator/agents/context.py">Read the model context code <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="privacy-grid">
<article><span class="privacy-number">01 / EXECUTION</span><h3>Work in your workspace</h3><p>DuckDB and Python run transformations in the environment where you host Datly, including a local installation on your computer.</p></article>
<article><span class="privacy-number">02 / MODEL CONTEXT</span><h3>Know what AI receives</h3><p>AI requests can include schema, sample values and rows, prompts, and tool outputs. A cloud model provider receives the context sent to it.</p></article>
<article><span class="privacy-number">03 / YOUR CONFIGURATION</span><h3>Choose your model</h3><p>Connect a supported provider or configure a local model through Ollama. Local AI requests require a local endpoint; remote data sources still need network access.</p></article>
</div></section>
<section class="setup-section section" id="quickstart" aria-labelledby="setup-heading"><div class="container setup-layout"><div class="setup-copy"><p class="eyebrow">06 / YOUR NEXT GOOD QUESTION</p><h2 id="setup-heading">A little setup.<br> A lot to explore.</h2><p>Build from source and launch Datly in your browser. Start with an example dataset, then configure a model for AI-assisted exploration.</p><ul class="requirements"><li>Python 3.11 or later</li><li>Node.js and Yarn for the frontend</li><li>uv for the Python environment</li></ul><div class="setup-links"><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/arpanjot-coding/Datly#readme">Read the setup guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/arpanjot-coding/Datly/releases">View releases <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-top"><div role="tablist" aria-label="Installation method"><button id="setup-tab-source" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="setup-code" tabindex="0" data-setup="source">From source</button><button id="setup-tab-docker" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="setup-code" tabindex="-1" data-setup="docker">Docker</button></div><button class="copy-button" id="copy-code" aria-label="Copy installation commands">Copy <span aria-hidden="true">⧉</span></button></div><pre id="setup-code" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="setup-tab-source" tabindex="0"><code># Get the project
git clone https://github.com/arpanjot-coding/Datly.git
cd Datly

# Build the interface
yarn
yarn build

# Install Python dependencies and launch
uv sync
uv run data_formulator</code></pre><div class="terminal-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span><span>Open <a href="http://localhost:5567">localhost:5567</a> after launch</span></div><p id="copy-status" class="sr-only" role="status"></p></div></div></section>
<section class="faq section container" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">A FEW THINGS TO KNOW</p><h2 id="faq-heading">Before you<br> dive in.</h2><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/arpanjot-coding/Datly/issues">Questions or feedback? <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="faq-items">
<details><summary>What is Datly?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Datly is an open-source analytics workspace built on Microsoft Research’s Data Formulator. It combines natural-language exploration, visual chart controls, data threads, table inspection, and editable reports. The project is in beta.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does my data stay on my computer?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>A local installation processes transformations on your computer. AI requests can include sample rows, field values, prompts, and tool outputs, which are sent to your configured model. If you select a cloud provider, that provider receives this context. A local model endpoint can keep model requests local; review your configuration and connected data sources.</p></details>
<details><summary>Does inspecting the table guarantee a correct answer?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No. The table helps you inspect the computed values, but AI can still choose an incorrect transformation, aggregation, or interpretation. Review the generated work, underlying data, and business assumptions before relying on a result.</p></details>
<details><summary>Which models can I use?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>The code includes support for OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, and local Ollama endpoints. You configure your provider and credentials. Model availability, capabilities, and any usage costs depend on that provider.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can I create reports and dashboards?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>You can edit rich-text reports with chart images, export a report image, or print to PDF. The current dashboard implementation saves chart references and displays title/type placeholders; a rendered, interactive multi-chart dashboard is not yet implemented.</p></details>
<details><summary>Is Datly free?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Datly’s source code is available under the MIT licence. Your model provider, connected infrastructure, or hosting may have their own costs. Check the repository’s setup instructions and releases for the current installation options.</p></details>
</div></section>
